  data: {
    LLM: 'Remember which tiles appeared where when they were turned face down so you can form pairs that total 12 on your turn.\n\nRemember the total (12) and think in terms of pairs of values (e.g. 7 and 5) rather than the two halves of each tile.\n\nDominoes do not break into simple pairs like cards: double-blank and double-six pair only with each other; 0-1 and 5-6 pair only with each other. For 7+5 there are nine tile combinations (1-6, 2-5, or 3-4 with 0-5, 1-4, or 2-3); use this to plan which positions to reveal.',
    Player: 'Remember which tiles appeared where when they were turned face down so you can form pairs that total 12 on your turn.\n\nRemember the total (12) and think in terms of pairs of values (e.g. 7 and 5) rather than the two halves of each tile.\n\nDominoes do not break into simple pairs like cards: double-blank and double-six pair only with each other; 0-1 and 5-6 pair only with each other. For 7+5 there are nine tile combinations (1-6, 2-5, or 3-4 with 0-5, 1-4, or 2-3); use this to plan which positions to reveal.',
